# Pages this archive draws by hand on purpose. checkpages prints every line
# of this file on every run, so an exemption nobody can justify is an
# exemption somebody will notice.

dna: 1,948 lines, and the only archive here whose DNA OVERTURNS THE PAPER RECORD. A haplogroup prediction against a result, a British cluster worked from the inside, triangulation on chromosome bands. Nothing on it is a shared object; it is a proof.
register: a register of PEOPLE, not of records, and the kit's Register component is emphatic that the distinction matters most on exactly this page — "a record is not a person and this page is where that matters most". This one lists everyone the source tree records, in two tables with sixty lines of argument between them: the family proper, and a hundred-odd people who share the surname and have not been shown to share anything else. The other seven list one row per record read. Putting people through a component built to count records would create the confusion it exists to prevent.
